  "report_summary": "In this Attack Library Scan, the target was tested for Brand, Safety and Security risks.\n\n### Summary\nThe application has low risk with an overall Risk Score of 8.72/100.\n\n### Key Risks and Implications\n\n**System Prompt Leakage:**\n- ASR 63.3% (167 successful attacks, including 35 critical/high severity, out of 264 total attempts). Attack patterns involve techniques to extract the underlying instructions governing the model's behavior.\n- The system's prompt hierarchy is insufficiently robust, allowing user input to effectively override or reveal core instructions, indicating a lack of clear separation between control and data.\n\n**Malicious Code Generation:**\n- ASR 15.3% (11 successful attacks, including 5 critical/high severity, out of 72 total attempts). Attack patterns involve prompting the model to generate code containing malicious functionality.\n- The model lacks sufficient signature recognition for common exploit patterns, enabling it to generate code that can be leveraged for cybercrime, revealing a weakness in its code analysis capabilities.\n\n\n### Recommendations\n\n**1. Enforcing Least Privilege Access Control**: \nGrant your AI application only the minimum permissions necessary to perform its intended functions. Limit access to sensitive resources, APIs, and data. Where possible, handle sensitive operations in your application code rather than allowing the model to access them directly.\n\n**2. Paraphrasing**: \nImplement paraphrasing techniques to disrupt potential manipulation attempts by restructuring and reinterpreting input data. By breaking up the original sequence and transforming the language, the system disrupts attempts to inject malicious instructions or hidden directives.\n\n**3. Runtime Execution Constraints**: \nSet time limits and resource consumption thresholds for code execution. Define maximum CPU usage, memory allocation, and execution duration. Terminate operations that exceed these limits to protect system resources and prevent excessive consumption.\n",
